WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.979
Today I want to talk about the online

00:00:01.979 --> 00:00:06.240
encyclopedia of integer sequences. This

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.970
is a website which has- it's a

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.170
database full of sequences. In fact the

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.150
the encyclopedia has been going

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.820
for nearly 50 years. It started in

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.680
book form and it's now grown into a big

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.130
website. So it started off by a guy

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.760
called Neil Sloane and he- for his

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.500
research he needed to- just needed to

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.240
tell some sequences apart and he wrote

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.640
them down. And he just started to collect

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.340
his sequences together, they were on to

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.100
punch cards - this is going back quite a

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.800
while now. And when the internet came

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.649
along that was a nice opportunity to put

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:47.149
it online and there are now two hundred

00:00:47.149 --> 00:00:51.420
thousand and more examples of integer

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.879
sequences. So there might be things like

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:57.350
the prime numbers, the square numbers;

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.690
just sequences with special properties.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.510
The classic example, right: 1729 is a

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.619
important number in mathematics, it's

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:12.299
part of mathematics folklore. 1729 was

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:14.640
the number that Ramanujan talked about

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.240
when Hardy asked him, you know, this is a

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.680
boring number can you think of anything

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.390
interesting about this number? And he

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.770
said 1729 is the smallest number that

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.930
can be written as the sum of two cubes

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.500
in two different ways. 
It's equal to 1 cubed

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.340
plus 12 cubed and it's equal to 9 cubed

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.119
plus 10 cubed, so that's an interesting,

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:41.670
that's interesting isn't it?
Now, in fact there are

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.970
other interesting things about 1729,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.380
that's not the only interesting thing

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.450
about it. And so it actually turns up in

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.119
other sequences - I'm just going to pull

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:53.100
out- this is the paper that I'm going to

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.000
refer to, in here it gives 1729 as an

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.560
example. So let's have a look,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.860
1729 is also the third Carmichael number,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.640
that means 1729 divides x to the power

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.050
1729 minus X

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.610
for all X. So whatever value X is it will

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.100
divide that perfectly - it's the third

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.710
Carmichael number. It's also the sixth

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:26.490
pseudo-prime number in base 2; which

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:35.550
means 1729 divides 2 to the power 1729

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.470
minus 1, minus 1. So there you 
go, so that's

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.870
called a pseudo-prime. It's the

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.820
thirteenth number that is one more than

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.830
a cube. And here the last example they've

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.520
got here I quite like this one: 1729 is

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.820
the total number of ways to express 33

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.410
as the sum of six integers. Again all

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.660
these different properties for

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.520
1729. The point

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.390
is, the more properties it has the more

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.090
sequences it appears in and I guess the

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.520
more interesting the number is. So the

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.660
more often it appears in the

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.210
encyclopedia of integers the more

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.070
interesting the numbers should be. So what

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.600
the guys who wrote this paper have done

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.510
is they counted how often each number

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.940
appeared in the encyclopedia of integers.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.520
And they looked at what pattern that

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.490
made. Now, try and think of what pattern

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.910
you think that might make, but this is

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:35.760
what they got. And you can see it's quite

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.520
clearly a curve, it decreases, it's a

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.860
curve. They found a little formula just

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.160
to describe that curve: this curve - I'll

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.120
write it on the paper -

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:52.760
is 253 million divided by N to the power

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.050
1.33. So that's the

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.080
equation of that curve. So these numbers

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.330
are appearing far more often in the

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.400
encyclopedia of integers, these turn up

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.590
really often and then the ones that are

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.130
lower down here appear less often. But

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.739
here's the interesting thing; you can

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:14.989
quite clearly see in this curve a gap

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:20.940
appearing. There is a
quite clear space here.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.190
And yet the numbers are actually forming

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.070
two bands. So there's a top band which is

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:26.780
filled with

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.550
the popular numbers, they're turning up

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.670
the most often, then this is huge gap and

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.310
then underneath there's a bottom band

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.950
there which are your less popular

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.930
numbers, they're the ordinary numbers

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.480
that aren't turning up so often. And

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.820
that's quite surprising. They could

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.220
generate this sort of curve if they had

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.500
a machine generating sequences and

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.600
integers, this is exactly the sort of

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.760
curve you would expect, so that wasn't a

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.250
surprise. The surprising thing was they

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:58.639
have this gap separating popular numbers

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:01.820
from the less popular numbers. Now the

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.610
ones in the top band are actually

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.640
numbers like the primes. All the primes

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.500
are there, in fact almost all the primes

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.490
are in the top band. About 95% of square

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.340
numbers are in the top band, in the

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.880
popular band. There are numbers like

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.310
numbers that are just perhaps 1 or-

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.919
plus 1 or minus 1 off a power of 2;

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:27.740
so that'd be 2 to the power n plus 1 or

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.950
2 to the power n minus 1. Or there might

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:32.900
be number such that have lots of factors;

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.360
numbers that factorise into lots of

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.820
little factors, those tend to appear in

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.760
the top band. Other numbers that appear

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.560
in the top band are ones that have

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.039
special properties in base 10; so numbers

00:05:46.039 --> 00:05:53.830
like 1111 are in the top band, 2222,

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:58.820
3333 all appear in the popular band of

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.039
numbers. So what they think is happening

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:03.979
here is obviously this is cultural - that

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:06.830
the popular numbers are the ones that

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.110
the mathematicians like. These are the

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.510
primes, or numbers that are related to

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.729
primes, and they are they are elevated

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:19.039
above the ordinary numbers and so they

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:20.690
have a special place all by themselves.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.360
And they're elevated by such an amount

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.180
that you can see a quite clear space

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.220
between them.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.620
